1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:16,920
These seven parallel Week Eight assignments explain mechanisms,
development, airway care and pharmacology within the school’s
pediatric and obstetric work. The other four required readings remain
with their teaching sessions; all eleven belong to this week.

2
00:00:16,920 --> 00:00:26,544
Anatomy and pathophysiology explain mechanisms. Fundamentals and
mental-health nursing help adapt observation and communication.

3
00:00:26,544 --> 00:00:38,088
Clinical procedures and medical-surgical nursing describe care
concepts in other settings. They are not automatic authority to
perform the same procedure in BC EMS.

4
00:00:38,088 --> 00:00:48,288
Pharmacology explains what uterine-motility classes do and why
monitoring matters; it does not turn a textbook drug table into a PCP
medication card.

5
00:00:48,288 --> 00:01:15,072
The actual course-folder Anatomy and Physiology second edition
resolves this row: printed pages 1185 to 1216 and 1223 to 1266 map to
physical pages 1201 to 1232 and 1239 to 1282. All seventy-six literal
pages and the ten named section concepts have now been reviewed. The
older candidate export had different pagination.

6
00:01:15,072 --> 00:01:37,848
The actual FON, MSN, Pharmacology and PMHN course-folder copies were
also checked. Read the literal page range alongside the named school
topic. Where they point to different material, both must be explained
rather than silently replacing one with the other. The separate
readers record the actual extent and the remaining source differences.

7
00:01:37,848 --> 00:02:05,976
The identified 2018 school-folder CPSC copy maps the assigned printed
pages six hundred ninety-five through seven hundred twenty-four to
intravenous therapy, while its named tracheostomy topic begins at page
eight hundred thirty-eight. Both contents have been reviewed and
explained. The book is identified; the remaining issue is the school’s
literal locator versus named topic, not an unknown adopted edition.

8
00:02:05,976 --> 00:02:17,160
Use each guide as a bilingual learning companion. When its locator
differs, verify the chapter and instructor’s copy rather than silently
substituting pages.

9
00:02:17,160 --> 00:02:29,136
A&P links ovarian function, pregnancy and fetal development. The
lesson focuses on how body systems adapt, not a memorized calendar of
guaranteed events.

10
00:02:29,136 --> 00:02:40,320
Congenital lesions alter pressure and flow in different ways. The same
structural label can have variable consequences depending on the
defect and developmental stage.

11
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:49,392
Respiratory and reproductive content helps explain why a pregnant
patient’s oxygenation, perfusion and airway need integrated
assessment.

12
00:02:49,392 --> 00:02:59,856
Use physiology to decide what question or observation is useful. Do
not use a classroom mechanism to diagnose a patient or replace a
current local pathway.

13
00:02:59,856 --> 00:03:10,128
Children develop skills in a broad sequence, but the timing varies.
Age is useful context; it is not a substitute for observing the child
in front of you.

14
00:03:10,128 --> 00:03:20,064
A child with intellectual, communication or autism-spectrum
differences may need a different way to signal pain, fear, consent or
distress.

15
00:03:20,064 --> 00:03:32,208
Ask what communication supports work and what the child usually does.
Include the child directly, and invite a caregiver’s observations with
the child’s safety and preferences in view.

16
00:03:32,208 --> 00:03:44,712
Do not assume a diagnosis explains every new symptom, or that distress
behavior is voluntary. Look for acute airway, breathing, seizure,
injury and perfusion threats.

17
00:03:44,712 --> 00:04:01,320
The CPSC reader explains the actual school-folder copy’s tracheostomy
material and the separate literal intravenous pages. Its identified-
copy evidence replaces the earlier candidate-only status, while the
genuine page-topic mismatch remains visible.

18
00:04:01,320 --> 00:04:11,736
CNS sections also span specimens, anthropometry and cardiopulmonary
assessment. They are not three equivalent tracheostomy-procedure
sections.

19
00:04:11,736 --> 00:04:21,408
Suction depth should be related to the actual tube or an individual
written plan. Do not borrow a generic fixed distance or push until
resistance.

20
00:04:21,408 --> 00:04:31,392
A text can inform your reasoning, but device manipulation and tube
replacement require the trained role, current protocol, equipment and
supervision.

21
00:04:31,392 --> 00:04:41,736
Oxytocin acts on uterine smooth muscle and is also part of the
physiology of milk ejection. The hormone’s roles differ by tissue and
context.

22
00:04:41,736 --> 00:04:50,040
Pharmacology texts compare medicines that increase uterine
contractions with agents used to slow them in selected circumstances.

23
00:04:50,040 --> 00:04:59,040
Hospital use can require continuous assessment because overly frequent
or sustained contractions may affect maternal and fetal status.

24
00:04:59,040 --> 00:05:09,072
The candidate textbook contains dosing tables for clinical settings.
They are deliberately not reproduced as an EMS or PCP administration
instruction.

25
00:05:09,072 --> 00:05:20,256
The school’s saved newborn page says forty to sixty assisted breaths
per minute. Current BCEHS M09 text and its linked algorithm specify a
different rate.

26
00:05:20,256 --> 00:05:36,840
BCPEG pages in the saved June 2026 snapshot also use a different
compression threshold from current M09/AHA neonatal guidance. The
existing newborn lesson labels the conflict and teaches the current
local service route.

27
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:48,408
For exam expectations, ask the instructor which revision is assessed
while keeping the current clinical distinction visible. Do not merge
rates or thresholds into a compromise.

28
00:05:48,408 --> 00:06:00,072
The same rule applies to pediatric glucose and device procedures:
record age, units, source and scope; never replace uncertainty with a
confident-looking number.

29
00:06:00,072 --> 00:06:09,768
Start from the school’s exact chapter and page locator. If the book
cover, edition or pagination differs, record that before taking notes.

30
00:06:09,768 --> 00:06:22,104
For each section, separate a mechanism, a finding, an action concept,
a caution and any role boundary. This makes it harder to mistake
background theory for a protocol.

31
00:06:22,104 --> 00:06:32,880
After reading, explain the point in your own English and Chinese words
rather than copying a paragraph. Keep units, dates and qualifiers
attached to the claim.

32
00:06:32,880 --> 00:06:43,752
Test one fresh example, then return to the source to correct your
explanation. A bilingual summary is a learning aid, not a substitute
for reading the assigned work.

33
00:06:43,752 --> 00:06:54,048
A congenital heart disease reading explains structural pathways, shunt
direction and changing resistance. Current symptoms still require
clinical assessment.

34
00:06:54,048 --> 00:07:04,080
Developmental and mental-health readings help adapt the questions to
the child and identify baseline communication. They do not erase the
child’s own report.

35
00:07:04,080 --> 00:07:14,184
The real tube, patient-specific plan and trained local procedure
determine a safe depth. A candidate nursing source cannot replace that
measurement.

36
00:07:14,184 --> 00:07:25,992
No. A medication table describes pharmacology in its source context.
Current service permission, indication, product and provider scope
remain separate requirements.

37
00:07:25,992 --> 00:07:50,832
The school links four historical articles as well as its module and
reading table. Their original bilingual guides are now included here.
Use them with the connected weekly lessons: neonatal illness after
birth, difficult delivery, pediatric assessment and basic airway
support. They are supplementary school readings, not four extra rows
in the eleven-row required textbook table.

38
00:07:50,832 --> 00:08:15,960
Read old clinical writing critically. It can explain a mechanism while
giving an outdated treatment or an action outside the local role. The
guides separate those questions. Learn the reason a finding matters,
then apply the current, age-appropriate and authorized pathway; do not
memorize an old article’s dose, oxygen target or invasive maneuver as
a school-endorsed field order.

39
00:08:15,960 --> 00:08:43,632
The assigned obstetrical video is now integrated with its original
bilingual guide. Follow its clinical sequence into the detailed
maternal assessment, delivery, newborn and Apgar lessons. A historical
overview can help organize the subject, but it cannot replace the
current decision gates or supervised skills practice. The full
available English narrative was read; that is not a claim that every
demonstrated technique was visually certified.

40
00:08:43,632 --> 00:09:13,776
Keep three distinctions explicit. First, a normal pregnancy adaptation
does not explain away new deterioration. Second, historical specialist
treatments do not create PCP authority. Third, the newborn’s response
to effective ventilation comes before the persistent bradycardia
compression branch. Use the source-specific guide to identify the old
shortcuts, then practise the connected original cases rather than
memorize the video’s closing answers.

41
00:09:13,776 --> 00:09:49,104
The four theories answer different questions. Erikson concerns
psychosocial tasks and relationships; Piaget concerns ways of
thinking; Kohlberg concerns the reasoning used to judge right and
wrong; Freud proposes historical psychosexual stages and personality
structures. Learn the names, sequence and explanatory purpose, then
apply them modestly. They are frameworks to improve questions and
support, not tests that classify a patient as successful, morally
inferior, incapable of consent or permanently damaged.

42
00:09:49,104 --> 00:10:27,576
Concrete operational thinking, approximately seven to eleven in this
source, supports logical reasoning about tangible events. Conservation
means that reshaping an equal amount does not change its quantity;
reversibility means mentally undoing a process; decentering means
considering more than one aspect or perspective. Formal operational
thinking, described from about twelve onward, permits hypothetical and
abstract reasoning. Stress, familiarity and individual development
influence performance. Neither age nor performance on one example
establishes decision-making capacity.

43
00:10:27,576 --> 00:11:04,728
Vaccination is administration of a vaccine; immunization is the
process of gaining protection. In active immunity, the person
generates an immune response and memory; passive protection supplies
antibodies made elsewhere, such as maternal antibodies or an
immunoglobulin product. Antigen and antibody are not synonyms: one is
a recognized target, the other a specific immune protein. Protection
depends on the product, host and time, so neither breastfeeding nor a
vaccination record guarantees that an unwell child cannot have an
infection.

44
00:11:04,728 --> 00:11:47,184
Before planned vaccination, verify the actual product, history, dose
schedule, contraindications, prior reactions and consent; explain
expected effects and serious warning signs. A catch-up plan is
product- and history-specific. The textbook US rule requiring guardian
consent for every non-emancipated minor must not replace BC assessment
of the particular young person and decision. Local mature-minor
conditions may permit the child to consent. Concerns about access,
cost, fear or trust deserve accurate information and practical
support, not punishment or withdrawal of unrelated care.

45
00:11:47,184 --> 00:12:30,600
Havighurst adds developmental tasks influenced by maturation, social
expectations and personal values. The source six stages are
infancy/early childhood zero to six, later childhood six to thirteen,
adolescence thirteen to eighteen, early adulthood eighteen to thirty,
middle age thirty to sixty and later maturity sixty onward. Examples
progress from walking, language and self-care to peer skills, values,
work, relationships, contribution and adapting to change. Its
historical gender-role, marriage and family assumptions are not
compulsory healthy-life requirements or a way to judge disability or
culture.

46
00:12:30,600 --> 00:13:08,376
Original integration: a school-age child who usually explains symptoms
clearly now gives short answers, sits apart and has new rapid
breathing. A peer conflict and an established learning difficulty may
be relevant, but neither explains away altered interaction or
breathing. Start with the physiological assessment and required
support, adapt questions to the child, obtain attributed caregiver
history and reassess response. Later prevention and referral can
address the wider context. The developmental framework helps the
assessment; it must not replace it.

47
00:13:08,376 --> 00:13:43,200
Oxytocin selectively promotes uterine smooth-muscle contraction,
increasing rhythmic activity and tone. In the breast, it helps milk
ejection by contracting myoepithelial cells; that is not the same as
milk production. The expected effect must match the indication: labor
contraction and postpartum uterine hemostasis are different clinical
settings. A drug name alone does not identify the route, concentration
or current order. An administered uterotonic does not eliminate the
need to assess ongoing bleeding and perfusion.

48
00:13:43,200 --> 00:14:27,000
Unit exercise, not a treatment order: if a simulation label contains
10 units in 1000 mL, it contains 0.01 unit per milliliter, or 10
milliunits per milliliter. If that simulation supplies a verified 1
milliunit per minute order, dividing 1 by 10 gives 0.1 milliliter per
minute, equivalent to 6 milliliters per hour. Units, milliunits,
milliliters and minutes are different quantities; confusing units with
milliunits creates a thousandfold error. The calculation does not
select a clinical dose, diluent, pump program or indication. Verify
the actual product and independently check the authorized plan.

49
00:14:27,000 --> 00:15:09,768
Magnesium depresses neuromuscular activity; excessive effect can
appear as weakness, reduced reflexes, reduced consciousness,
hypotension or inadequate breathing. Renal impairment can increase
accumulation, and other depressant medicines can complicate
assessment. The fetus or newborn can also be affected. Follow the
actual monitoring and escalation plan rather than calling quietness
successful treatment. Calcium is a specialist countermeasure for
significant toxicity, not an independent PCP dose derived here. Assess
and support the actual airway, breathing and circulation while
urgently communicating the suspected problem.

50
00:15:09,768 --> 00:15:50,712
Original transfer case: a postpartum patient receiving a reported
magnesium infusion becomes quieter, weaker and has shallower
breathing. Quietness does not prove seizure prevention is safely
achieved, and the medicine is not the only possible cause. Reassess
airway, ventilation, circulation and responsiveness; verify the actual
bag, concentration, pump history and units, and alert the responsible
clinical team urgently. Provide indicated support within the actual
emergency plan. Do not independently escalate an infusion or diagnose
toxicity only from the drug name.

51
00:15:50,712 --> 00:16:27,048
Expressive communication sends meaning; receptive communication
receives and understands it. A child who cannot speak clearly may
understand a great deal, while fluent speech does not guarantee
understanding. Ask one concrete question, allow processing time, and
offer the child's own effective method: speech, gesture, writing,
pictures or a communication device. Check understanding by asking the
child to show or explain the next step. Do not treat a communication
aid as evidence that the child cannot decide or feel pain.

52
00:16:27,048 --> 00:17:06,576
Sensory input and uncertainty can make an assessment overwhelming:
touch, alarms, bright light, crowds or a sudden change may matter. Ask
the child and caregiver what usually helps, reduce avoidable
stimulation and explain before touching. Repetitive movement may help
self-regulation rather than represent deliberate disruption. Support a
preferred communication method and acknowledge strengths without
assuming every autistic child has the same talent. These adaptations
accompany assessment of breathing, perfusion, injury and other new
illness; they do not replace it.

53
00:17:06,576 --> 00:17:40,680
Motor tics are repeated movements; vocal tics are repeated sounds.
Tourette syndrome involves a history of multiple motor tics and at
least one vocal tic, with a course lasting at least a year and onset
before eighteen; the motor and vocal tics need not occur together.
Persistent motor or vocal tic disorder involves one type rather than
both; provisional tic disorder has a shorter course. These
distinctions describe formal assessment, not diagnosis by one observed
twitch. Coprolalia is not required.

54
00:17:40,680 --> 00:18:18,432
Original case: a twelve-year-old who usually uses short spoken phrases
begins repeatedly turning away and pushing an assessment hand aside. A
familiar caregiver reports this is new and the child is less
responsive than usual. Reduce noise, explain one step and retain the
child's communication support, while promptly assessing airway,
breathing, perfusion, responsiveness and other indicated causes.
Document the change and response. A developmental label does not
explain the new decline, and forcing eye contact does not establish
that the child is safe.

55
00:18:18,432 --> 00:18:57,504
In bronchiectasis, damaged bronchial walls remain abnormally widened.
Wider does not mean easier clearance: retained secretions can
encourage infection, inflammation and further wall injury, creating a
self-reinforcing cycle. The assigned page names cystic fibrosis,
ciliary dysfunction, previous infection, immune problems and
obstruction among associated causes or risks; some cases are
unexplained. Assess the actual history and change, not sex or age
stereotypes. A chronic diagnosis does not establish the cause of
today's breathlessness.

56
00:18:57,504 --> 00:19:34,056
The pneumonia page shows inflammation and alveolar filling reducing
the normally open gas-exchange space. Infection and aspiration can
both injure lung tissue, but aspiration inflammation is not
automatically proof of a bacterial infection needing the same drug.
Ask about onset, aspiration risk, fever, secretions, breathing work
and clinical trend. Low reserve, fatigue or new reduced responsiveness
matters. Explain the mechanism without turning a picture into a
diagnosis or delaying indicated respiratory support while waiting for
fever.

57
00:19:34,056 --> 00:20:16,560
Pelvic symptoms overlap. PID may involve infection-associated
pain/discharge; endometriosis often has a cyclical pain history;
fibroids may produce bleeding or pressure. These are patterns for
reasoning, not mutually exclusive field diagnoses. Ask about timing,
bleeding, pregnancy possibility, recent birth/procedures, fever, pain,
urinary symptoms, medicines and the person's concerns. Privacy,
consent, relevant language and a nonjudgmental explanation improve the
history. Acute perfusion or breathing threats take priority over
completing an intimate checklist.

58
00:20:16,560 --> 00:20:56,976
Original case: a postpartum patient reports a previous fibroid
diagnosis and new heavier bleeding, dizziness and weakness. Do not let
the familiar diagnosis settle the cause. Assess current
responsiveness, airway, breathing and perfusion; clarify recent
birth/procedures, the bleeding trend and the actual treatment plan;
provide indicated support and urgent communication. The literal pelvic
reading explains overlapping symptoms, while the separate maternal-
hemorrhage pathway governs the obstetric response. Improvement must be
demonstrated by reassessment, not assumed after an intervention.

59
00:20:56,976 --> 00:21:30,504
Count chromosome sets separately from DNA copies. A primary gametocyte
is diploid; after meiosis I, a secondary gametocyte is already haploid
even though each chromosome still has two chromatids. Meiosis II
separates those chromatids. Thus a secondary oocyte has twenty-three
duplicated chromosomes, not a diploid forty-six-chromosome set. The
source prose calls it diploid, but its own figure marks it n.
Fertilization normally restores two chromosome sets in the zygote.

60
00:21:30,504 --> 00:22:08,184
Oogenesis begins before birth. Primary oocytes pause in meiosis I; a
selected one completes that division before ovulation and produces a
large secondary oocyte plus a small polar body. The secondary oocyte
pauses in metaphase II and completes the division after fertilization-
related activation. Unequal division retains most cytoplasm in one
cell; it does not make four equivalent eggs. Contrast this with four
spermatids from one primary spermatocyte, and keep nuclear inheritance
separate from mainly oocyte-derived cytoplasm/mitochondria.

61
00:22:08,184 --> 00:22:43,848
FSH supports follicular development while ovarian steroids and inhibin
influence feedback. Much of the cycle uses negative feedback.
Sustained high estradiol near the appropriate pre-ovulatory stage
instead promotes positive feedback and the LH surge. LH drives oocyte
maturation, follicular rupture and luteinization. After ovulation,
progesterone and estrogen from the corpus luteum support the
endometrium and suppress another immediate recruitment cycle. A
model's day fourteen is not a guaranteed ovulation date.

62
00:22:43,848 --> 00:23:19,176
Match the two clocks: ovarian follicular phase overlaps uterine
menstruation and proliferation; ovarian luteal phase overlaps uterine
secretion. Estrogen helps rebuild the functional endometrium;
progesterone supports its secretory state and vascular preparation.
Without pregnancy-support signaling, luteal regression lowers steroid
support and the functional layer is shed. Menarche is the first
menstrual bleeding, but it is not a guarantee that every early cycle
is ovulatory or that pregnancy was impossible before that first bleed.

63
00:23:19,176 --> 00:23:57,864
Maternal blood normally flows around placental villi while fetal blood
stays in villous vessels; exchange occurs across tissue interfaces.
Normal separation is not an absolute ban on cell transfer. Fetal red
cells can enter maternal circulation and sensitization can matter,
including after prior pregnancy events or other exposures. Risk
depends on antibodies and exposure history, not birth order alone. Rh
status, relevant events and prior prevention belong in clinical
history and specialist follow-up, not an independently selected
prophylaxis schedule here.

64
00:23:57,864 --> 00:24:26,640
The umbilical vein brings relatively oxygen-rich blood from the
placenta toward the fetus. Some flow passes through the liver and some
through the ductus venosus toward the inferior vena cava. The foramen
ovale connects right and left atria; the ductus arteriosus connects
the pulmonary arterial pathway to the aorta. These shunts distribute
flow around a high-resistance lung circuit; they do not mean the fetal
liver receives no meaningful blood flow.

65
00:24:26,640 --> 00:25:03,600
Pregnancy increases blood volume, cardiac workload and ventilation,
while vascular and mechanical changes alter the examination. Do not
copy the source claim that blood pressure must rise or each
inspiration becomes smaller: pressure can fall in mid-pregnancy, and
tidal volume/minute ventilation generally increase despite reduced
functional residual capacity. Reduced reserve helps explain rapid
respiratory deterioration. Severe headache, new breathlessness,
bleeding, syncope or poor perfusion still requires assessment rather
than being called normal pregnancy.

66
00:25:03,600 --> 00:25:37,632
Original synthesis: at a birth rehearsal, one learner says 'umbilical
artery means oxygen-rich', another says 'all shunts close permanently
with the first breath', and a third wants to wait for an Apgar total
before supporting poor breathing. Correct the chain: name vessels by
direction, trace placental gas exchange and the three shunts, explain
gradual pressure/flow transition, and act on the newborn's actual
breathing/heart rate through the current pathway. Keep a separate
maternal reassessment running throughout.

67
00:25:37,632 --> 00:26:13,416
Meiosis segregates alleles into gametes. In a simple complete-
dominance model, cross two heterozygotes, each carrying one capital-G
allele and one lowercase-g allele. The possibilities are two capital
alleles, two mixed combinations, or two lowercase alleles. The
genotype ratio is one to two to one; with complete dominance, the
phenotype ratio is three to one. Independent assortment has linkage
exceptions. These are probabilities for each conception, not a
required order or exact number of affected children.

68
00:26:13,416 --> 00:26:48,264
In a simplified X-linked model, an XY parent's X goes to XX offspring
and the Y to XY offspring, so an X-linked variant is not transmitted
father-to-son by that X. With a heterozygous XX parent and an
unaffected XY parent, each child has a one-half chance of receiving
the variant-bearing maternal X; the consequence depends on the child's
chromosomes and the disorder. Do not turn the source's 'unaffected
carrier' simplification into a clinical rule: females with hemophilia-
related variants can bleed.

69
00:26:48,264 --> 00:27:24,072
Use the complete Chapter Six reading to trace blood through each
connection. An atrial or ventricular septal defect can add pulmonary
flow and volume load; the amount depends on the defect and relative
resistance. Coarctation obstructs systemic outflow and can threaten
lower-body perfusion. Tetralogy combines outflow obstruction, a
ventricular septal defect, an overriding aorta and right-ventricular
hypertrophy. Describe pressure and direction rather than assuming
every congenital lesion has the same cyanosis or saturation.

70
00:27:24,072 --> 00:27:55,680
An infant who initially appeared well can deteriorate as transitional
circulation changes. Compare feeding, breathing effort, alertness and
limb perfusion with the actual baseline. Cyanosis is not required
before a major defect becomes dangerous, and an isolated murmur or
weak pulse cannot establish anatomy. Assess immediate threats and use
the current pediatric support and escalation pathway while keeping
infection and respiratory causes in the differential.

71
00:27:55,680 --> 00:28:32,328
The complete chapter includes eight defect sections, not only the
familiar five names. In d-transposition, the two circuits run in
parallel and effective mixing is essential. A patent ductus can cause
excess pulmonary flow in one lesion yet support mixing or systemic
supply in another. Complete AV canal combines atrial and ventricular
defects with an abnormal common atrioventricular junction and valve.
Truncus arteriosus has one arterial outlet, with flow distribution and
possible valve leakage adding to the burden.

72
00:28:32,328 --> 00:29:02,424
For retrieval, contrast a large ventricular shunt with parallel
circulation, then explain why an open duct is not universally good or
bad. Ask about the actual diagnosis, operations, medicines and usual
observations without inventing a target. Maintaining or closing a duct
is specialist care, not a new PCP drug instruction. Hand over the
deterioration and the evidence instead of delaying support to obtain a
perfect anatomical label.
